## Proposed repair plan
# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:home.spec.ts::home.spec.ts_>_home_page_>_shows_the_store_name_as_its_heading

## Diagnosis

The failing assertion is a straightforward production content defect, not a
structural or wiring problem.

- `tests/playwright.config.ts` starts the service itself (`webServer.command:
  node ../services/web/server.js`) on a path-derived port and sets `baseURL`
  to the same port, so the page under test is unambiguously served by
  `services/web/server.js`. No fixture, proxy or build step sits in between.
- `services/web/server.js` serves a single hard-coded HTML string for every
  request. Its `<h1>` element contains the literal text `Welcome`.
- `tests/integration/home.spec.ts:9` reads `page.locator('h1').textContent()`
  once (no retry/auto-wait on text) and requires it to equal exactly
  `Corner Shop`.

So `received: "Welcome"` is literally the `<h1>` body in the served markup.
The source file even carries a comment on lines 1-3 naming this as the planted
defect in production source. The contract the test states is: the home page's
single top-level heading is the store's name, `Corner Shop` — not a generic
greeting.

## The one change

**File: `services/web/server.js` (the only declared write root for service
`web`, per `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` → `services[0].path:
services/web`).**

In the `page` template literal, change the heading element's text content from
`Welcome` to `Corner Shop`, so the markup reads:

    <h1>Corner Shop</h1>

Requirements on the edit:

- The text node must be exactly `Corner Shop` — one space, that
  capitalisation, and **no surrounding whitespace or newlines inside the `h1`
  element**. `textContent()` returns the raw text node verbatim, and the
  assertion is `toBe`, not a trimmed or substring match. Writing the element
  across multiple lines (e.g. `<h1>\n  Corner Shop\n</h1>`) would return
  `"\n  Corner Shop\n"` and keep the test red. Keep it on a single line
  exactly as the current `Welcome` heading is.
- Keep it a single `h1` in the document. The locator is the bare `h1`
  selector; adding a second `h1` anywhere in the page would make the locator
  strict-mode-ambiguous and fail with a different error.
- Do not change anything else in the served markup. In particular leave the
  `<a href="/hours">Opening hours</a>` element exactly as it is, and leave
  `<title>Corner Shop</title>` alone (the title already says the store name;
  it is not what the test reads, and it is not the defect).
- Do not touch the server plumbing: the `http.createServer` handler, the
  `Content-Type` header, the `200` status, or
  `.listen(Number(process.env.PORT), '127.0.0.1')`. The harness depends on the
  server binding the `PORT` env var on loopback and answering `/` with a 200,
  because `webServer.url` polling is how Playwright decides the service is up.
  Any change there risks a start-up timeout rather than a passing test.
- Optionally update the now-stale comment block on lines 1-3 of
  `services/web/server.js`, which describes the heading as a planted defect
  still present. This is cosmetic and safe (it is production source, not a
  document under `docs/` or `true-bdd/`); it is not required for the test to
  pass, and the writer may skip it. No other comment or code edits.

No new files, no new dependencies, no config change. The repair is confined to
one line of one existing production file.

